WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Collingwood Council endorsed a 19-goal climate plan to steer budgets against heavier rain and heat. This is Collingwood Weekly, Episode 4. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. I don't live in Collingwood. Ontario Construction News says the plan also covers flooding, shoreline erosion, and warmer winters. It is split into people and community, services and operations, and the natural environment. Mayor Hamlin said in a statement that this is practical planning for whether the town is already getting.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.120
She said starting early protects residents, property, and everyday services and can cut

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.580
the bill for emergency repairs.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:41.220
Staff, regional partners, community feedback, local climate data, and technical analysis

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.820
set the risks and the responses.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.520
The endorsement puts the plan into work plans, budgets, and asset management, and every project

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.680
still has to pass the regular budget process.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.980
It also asks for tighter emergency planning, plus plain information people can use before

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:56.640
a bad storm.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.180
The town wants households to write an emergency plan, check drainage, clear

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.374
eva-strofs, and look-in on neighbors. A wasaga beach resident is now charged in a

00:01:06.374 --> 00:01:12.034
march fire at the Villanova Motel, Barry 360 reports. Ontario Provincial Police say

00:01:12.034 --> 00:01:15.814
crews reach the Mosley Street building shortly before midnight on March 9th.

00:01:16.194 --> 00:01:20.274
Everyone got out and nobody was hurt. Police arrested a 58-year-old from

00:01:20.274 --> 00:01:24.954
wasaga beach this week. The charges are arson, disregard for human life, and

00:01:24.954 --> 00:01:26.554
and failing to comply with an undertaking.

00:01:27.214 --> 00:01:29.874
The accused is due in court on October 27th.

00:01:30.234 --> 00:01:32.494
Calling Wood's election count, from Calling Wood today,

00:01:32.814 --> 00:01:35.914
is 2010 ballots in the first week of internet voting.

00:01:36.998 --> 00:01:39.058
Blue Mountain Resort has a winter opening day,

00:01:39.658 --> 00:01:41.058
CKNX News Today reports.

00:01:41.678 --> 00:01:44.638
The 2026-27 ski and snowboard season

00:01:44.638 --> 00:01:47.658
starts Thursday, December 10th, at 8.30 in the morning.

00:01:48.138 --> 00:01:51.078
The resort said crews are finishing the slopes, testing lifts,

00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:52.998
and getting the hill ready for first turns.

00:01:52.998 --> 00:01:57.938
That date is one day later than last season, which opened December 9th and ran through April

00:01:57.938 --> 00:01:58.738
22nd.

00:01:59.138 --> 00:02:03.258
The hill near Collingwood is Ontario's largest ski and snowboard destination.

00:02:03.858 --> 00:02:07.818
Visitors are being pointed to the resort website for trail conditions and hours as that

00:02:07.818 --> 00:02:08.758
morning gets closer.

00:02:09.905 --> 00:02:13.785
Wessaga Beach has machines on the ground for a bigger parking lot at the Wessaga Stars

00:02:13.785 --> 00:02:15.445
Arena and the public library.

00:02:15.905 --> 00:02:20.825
The town notice says JB Enterprises started tree clearing and earthworks on October 15th,

00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:21.685
whether permitting.

00:02:21.685 --> 00:02:25.845
Town staff are handling contract administration and construction inspection.

00:02:26.425 --> 00:02:30.305
Neighbors should expect noise, equipment, and truck traffic in permitted working hours.

00:02:30.825 --> 00:02:34.285
Questions go to Mike Pincivero, Manager of Engineering Services.

00:02:35.465 --> 00:02:39.345
Gray County is collecting snowplow names at the council table's new public site,

00:02:39.745 --> 00:02:42.005
Connect Gray, the Owen Sound Current Reports.

00:02:42.625 --> 00:02:46.165
Suggestions close October 22nd at 11.59 at night.

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:51.085
One person can send in up to three names, and this contest is the site's first project.

00:02:51.085 --> 00:02:55.625
A short list goes to a public vote from October 23rd through November 6th.

00:02:55.985 --> 00:03:00.005
Winning names are announced November 10th for plows in each of four patrol areas.

00:03:00.405 --> 00:03:04.845
Four top entries get prize packs and only a registered account with an email can claim

00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:05.885
credit or a prize.

00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:11.065
If two people send the same name, only the first one counts and every name has to be fine

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:11.805
for all ages.

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:16.245
Puns on winter, snow, plowing or the area are what the county wants.

00:03:16.245 --> 00:03:24.103
Gray County's Committee of the Whole voted October 8th to back a push to hold a rural infrastructure fund at $400 million a year.

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:31.023
The Owen Sound Current says Gray County and Owen Sound together received $7.5 million from that fund in 2026.

00:03:31.643 --> 00:03:36.023
The money is for roads, bridges, and water, and the five-year doubling expires this year.

00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:44.043
The vote supports a June 25 letter from the Municipal Finance Officers Association of Ontario to Acting Infrastructure Minister Todd McCarthy.

00:03:44.043 --> 00:03:51.903
That letter asks Ontario to lock the doubled Ontario Community Infrastructure Fund in before it calculates 2027 allocations.

00:03:52.323 --> 00:03:59.143
The province lifted the fund from about $200 million a year to $400 million, starting with 2022 allocations.

00:03:59.523 --> 00:04:03.923
The county resolution notes no public promise to keep that level past 2026.

00:04:04.543 --> 00:04:13.003
Gray County's own allocation was $1,279,946 in 2021, the last year before the doubling.

00:04:13.003 --> 00:04:26.263
It was $2,645,238 in 2022. It reached $3,836,916 in 2026. Owen Sound went from

00:04:26.263 --> 00:04:36.243
about $1.1 million in 2021 to $3,647,845 in 2026. The deputy treasurer told

00:04:36.243 --> 00:04:40.943
the committee the $400,000,000 is a fixed pool re-weighted every year. The weight

00:04:40.943 --> 00:04:45.103
uses the condition and replacement value of assets in each asset management plan.

00:04:45.523 --> 00:04:49.343
He said Gray County's larger share has effectively come out of other municipalities

00:04:49.343 --> 00:04:52.763
because the province has not raised the pool's total in five years.

00:04:53.563 --> 00:04:56.663
2026 allocations were announced in September 2025.

00:04:57.917 --> 00:05:01.357
At the Association of Municipalities of Ontario Conference in August,

00:05:01.797 --> 00:05:06.257
McCarthy was asked about keeping the doubled fund. He pointed to investments already made,

00:05:06.617 --> 00:05:10.317
including a new stream for municipalities that do not charge development charges,

00:05:10.317 --> 00:05:14.877
and he did not commit to 400 million. That account comes from a report by the

00:05:14.877 --> 00:05:19.497
Association of Municipal Managers, clerks and treasurers of Ontario. Council

00:05:19.497 --> 00:05:23.797
Eccles pulled the item off the consent agenda. He argued 400 million falls

00:05:23.797 --> 00:05:28.077
well short once it is split among every eligible community. He said he wants the

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:32.737
fund raised every year so it keeps pace with costs. CIO Randy Scherzer said

00:05:32.737 --> 00:05:36.037
council has already asked the province to account for inflation and municipal

00:05:36.037 --> 00:05:40.117
funding. He said an asset management update at the October 22nd

00:05:40.117 --> 00:05:44.057
committee of the whole will include new figures on Gray County's infrastructure needs.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:49.637
Council Greg said Bionterio rules now apply to municipalities and tend to raise prices

00:05:49.637 --> 00:05:51.717
because they require domestic purchasing.

00:05:52.237 --> 00:05:55.557
He said that price pressure strengthens the case for more provincial funding.

00:05:56.157 --> 00:06:00.557
Municipal affairs and housing minister Rob Flax wrote heads of council on September 15

00:06:00.857 --> 00:06:05.917
that failing to comply with Bionterio will force the province to reconsider funding made available

00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:06.817
to municipalities.

00:06:06.817 --> 00:06:10.657
the committee received that letter for information and did not discuss it.

00:06:11.157 --> 00:06:15.477
Scherzer said staff will contact the Finance Officers Association about its next steps.

